<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virji Vora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1590–c. 1670</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virji Vora was a merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> in Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>'s records describe as the richest merchant in the world at the time, with a personal worth estimated at 8 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. A wholesale trader, moneylender and banker with agents across India and in the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, he often bought up the whole stock of a commodity such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> or coral and dictated its price. He was the greatest creditor of the English Company, and much of the capital lent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> company in India came from him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s raid on Surat in 1664 looted his house and warehouses; he is last recorded in 1670.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Surat, the empire’s great port, <b>Virji Vora</b> traded in pepper, bullion, coral and opium through agents from Agra to Calicut, lent to the English and Dutch companies, and was said by the English to be worth 8 million rupees.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
